# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-subjection-2.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: mill-subjection-2.logic.r1 and mill-subjection-2.logic.r1.3 have "rests_with_women"; mill-subjection-2.logic.r1.2 has nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
mill-subjection-2.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered yes, which keeps mill-subjection-2.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "the question rests with women themselves, to be decided by their own experience and the use of their own faculties" is false. Is S3, "For, according to all the principles involved in modern society, the question rests with women themselves--to be decided by their own experience, and by the use of their own faculties.", true then?
mill-subjection-2.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: mill-subjection-2.logic.r1 has "only_by_trying" and "¬others_can_discover"; mill-subjection-2.logic.r1.2 and mill-subjection-2.logic.r1.3 have "only_by_trying" and "¬◇others_can_discover"; they differ when only "there are no means of finding what either one person or many can do but by trying" and "possibly, someone else can discover for them what it is for their happiness to do or leave undone" are true, which the first allows
mill-subjection-2.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S4 is read mill-subjection-2.logic.r1 with no modality; mill-subjection-2.logic.r1.2 and mill-subjection-2.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "can" does not say which it means
mill-subjection-2.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered yes, which keeps mill-subjection-2.logic.r1, to: Suppose "there are no means of finding what either one person or many can do but by trying", "possibly, someone else can discover for them what it is for their happiness to do or leave undone" and "possibly, someone else can discover for them what it is for their happiness to do or leave undone" are true and "someone else can discover for them what it is for their happiness to do or leave undone" is false. Is S4, "There are no means of finding what either one person or many can do, but by trying--and no means by which any one else can discover for them what it is for their happiness to do or leave undone.", true then?
mill-subjection-2.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: mill-subjection-2.logic.r1 has "rests_with_women ∧ only_by_trying ∧ ¬others_can_discover → ¬knowledge_necessary"; mill-subjection-2.logic.r1.2 has "only_by_trying ∧ ¬◇others_can_discover → rests_with_women" and "rests_with_women → ¬knowledge_necessary"; they differ when only "such knowledge is necessary for some practical purpose connected with the position of women in relation to society and life", "the question rests with women themselves, to be decided by their own experience and the use of their own faculties", "there are no means of finding what either one person or many can do but by trying" and "someone else can discover for them what it is for their happiness to do or leave undone" are true, which the first allows; mill-subjection-2.logic.r1.3 has "rests_with_women → ¬knowledge_necessary"
